Let the search for the echo's delay learn in the near end's pauses when talk there keeps it from finding a weak echo

With the near end talking from the start of a call (speech with short pauses) over an echo of
speech some 15 to 25 dB under the far end, the matched filters that search for the delay never
match the capture well enough to count (error near 0.7 of the capture, the threshold is 0.2):
the near end's talk, louder than the echo, keeps pulling them off it, and their peak jumps
around. No delay is found, the transparent mode takes the call for a headset after about 6 s,
and the echo goes through at 0 dB in the near end's pauses for as long as both keep talking.
Measured in the AEC3 simulator (M158 with main's patches and 0017 and 0018 of this series,
16 kHz): echo of 200 ms at -15 to -25 dB, near end at two levels, 3 seeds each: in 11 of 18
runs the echo in the pauses from 8 to 20 s stays within 6 dB of what comes in (median -3 dB).
Given the right delay from outside, the same runs reach -5 to -25 dB: that is as far as AEC3
goes with this much double talk.

When no delay has been found after 5 s of render and the filters matched the capture fewer
than 50 times by then, they now adapt, until a delay is found or they are reset, only on
updates whose capture, against the loudest render within their reach, is no more than 6 dB
above the lowest fifth of that ratio over the last 2 s: the near end's pauses. There the echo
is what the capture holds, the filters find it, the delay follows and the linear filter takes
over. Digital silence (a muted microphone) is left out of those ratios, and the filters are
held only while the capture of those 2 s shows pauses (more than 10 dB between its lowest and
highest fifths): a steady sound at the near end has no pauses to learn in, and holding there
moved the echo window by window when it appeared some seconds into the call. Starting at 1.5 s
instead also moved the delay's finding, and with it the near end's level by up to 10 dB in a
2 s window either way, in double talk with a strong echo where the delay comes on its own after
some seconds, and in unmutes with an echo shorter than the headroom; at 5 s those are as before.

Simulator, same runs: the echo in the pauses from 8 to 20 s stays within 6 dB in 4 of 18 runs
(median -13 dB; best runs -20 to -30 dB); the near end loses 0.6 dB more on average over
those seconds (2.2 dB in the worst run, whose echo goes from -10 to -17 dB), as much as AEC3
takes from it in double talk once it cancels an echo (near end starting at 4 s: -1 to -4 dB).
With an echo of 60 ms, which main finds later, the echo is the same or lower and the near end
moves up to 3 dB in a 2 s window either way, the same on average but for one run (0.6 dB
less, its echo 2.4 dB lower). Identical: music, the start of a call with music or speech on
speakers or a headset (also quiet music, -30 dB at 200 ms), double talk where a delay is found
by 5 s (40 seeds), an echo shorter than the headroom with 0017, a headset's leak with 0018,
the unmute series (4 setups, 48 seeds each), and an echo that appears 4 to 7 s into a call
with a steady tone at the near end.

